%I A400802 #5 Oct 12 2026 20:10:37
%S A400802 0,1,1,5,1,11,3,13,4,23,3,27,5,21,7,35,5,39,8,27,9
%N A400802 Number of orbits of exponents d, 1 <= d <= 2^n-2, under multiplication by 2 modulo 2^n-1, such that the power map x -> x^d on GF(2^n) is almost perfect nonlinear (APN).
%C A400802 The power map x^d is APN if for every b in GF(2^n) the equation (x+1)^d + x^d = b has at most 2 solutions x in GF(2^n).
%C A400802 The number of exponents d, 1 <= d <= 2^n-2, for which x^d is APN is n*a(n), since every such orbit has exactly n elements.
%F A400802 Conjecture: for even n, a(n) = phi(n) - 1 + [5|n]; for odd n >= 11, a(n) = 2*phi(n) + 3 + 2*[5|n], where [5|n] = 1 if 5 divides n and 0 otherwise. These formulas agree with all terms for n >= 10.
%e A400802 a(3) = 1: the exponents 3, 5, 6 form one orbit modulo 7, and x^3 is APN on GF(8).
%o A400802 (PARI) a(n) = {my(M=2^n-1, g=ffprimroot(ffgen(2^n, 'z)), X=concat([0*g], vector(M, i, g^i)), c=0, e, ok); for(d=1, M-1, e=d; ok=1; for(i=1, n-1, e=2*e%M; if(e<d, ok=0; break)); if(!ok, next); if(#Set(vector(2^n, i, (X[i]+1)^d+X[i]^d))==2^(n-1), c++)); c}
%K A400802 nonn,more
%O A400802 2,4
%A A400802 _Sajid Khan Hussain_, Oct 06 2026
